|  There is no record that the family court judge even forwarded these transcripts
|  to the appellate court, as required by New York law; New York Family Court Act
|  § 664(b), Civil Practice Law & Rules § 4019; see Fleishman v. Walters, 40 A.D.2d 622. In theory, an appellate court should not accept a family court custody ruling
|  if it is based more on private interviews than on evidence taken in court; People
|  ex rel. Kessler v. Cotter, 285 a.d. 206. In practice, appellate courts rarely give
.  family court decisions close scrutiny.39


