r#  36556  688  3
p: .
t. 
|  An appellate judge would describe the earlier decision this way: [The judge]
|  questioned her motivations, her credibility and her mental stability. It is clear
|  that he did not believe any part of plaintiff ’s allegations of abuse involving the
|  daughter. His findings were based upon a fully developed record following an
|  extensive evidentiary hearing. (Memorandum decision, March 28, 1995.) This
|  confirms that the real record of a case like this is not the official legal one, for as
|  a matter of fact the abuse allegations had never been resolved by the court; they
|  were withdrawn when the father agreed to forgo all contact with the girl. Nor
|  did the earlier judge ever actually conclude that the charges had been false. The
|  fully developed record so calmly referred to by the appellate judge simply did
|  not exist in any legal sense. The record had been written, so to speak, between
.  the lines.65


